# round_nonnumber_contract_v06.im -- round() with a non-number argument has two
# answers, and this pin asserts each platform's OWN answer instead of picking a
# winner.
#
#   POSIX (posix_core_round, src/runtime/runtime_posix.c:104)  -> nil
#   WIN32 (builtin_round,    src/runtime/runtime.c:41)         -> throws
#          "round: expected number"
#
# REGISTERED, NOT RESOLVED.  Neither answer is the baseline: both runtimes are
# consumers of a contract that does not exist yet (docs/DECFY_DESIGN.md:8-12 --
# "让所有后端消费同一个 IR").  Precedent: docs/AUDIT.md 1.25 -- sum() with a
# non-numeric element answers nil on POSIX and throws on WIN32, and that ruling
# kept both styles and recorded the divergence rather than unifying it.  Class:
# docs/SYNTAX.md 7.1 D5 -- a bad argument answered with nil is a silent wrong
# answer, while the throw is loud; by 7's own classification the loud one is
# better, but "better" is not "decided".
#
# provenance (NOT a rationale): the WIN32 copy carries an explicit check whose
# author and date are not recoverable; the POSIX copy has none.  The design
# record has no convention here -- "expected number" has zero hits in it, and
# there is no general argument-type doctrine (see docs/AUDIT.md 1.46).
#
# The two answers are asserted in CMakeLists.txt, one per platform, so the
# divergence is visible and change-detectable without adjudicating it.
#
# Values only, never exit codes.

r = round("x", 2)
say "round-nonnumber=" + str(r)
